<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>The image of God</h4>
<p>It is important to explain that &laquo;image of God&raquo; does not mean God has a human body. It means we share divine attributes: intelligence, free will, capacity to love. Use the analogy of a seal that leaves its mark on wax: the mark is not the seal, but it bears its shape.</p>

<h4>Dignity vs. self-esteem</h4>
<p>Dignity does not depend on how we feel. A person can have low self-esteem but retain all their dignity. Help distinguish between objective value (what we are before God) and subjective value (what we feel). The Gospel invites us to know our objective dignity in order to heal our self-esteem.</p>

<h4>Original sin</h4>
<p>Be careful when discussing original sin: it is not personal guilt, but an inherited condition that inclines us to evil. It does not mean we are bad by nature; it means we are wounded. Christ has redeemed and restored us. The tone should be hopeful, not condemnatory.</p>

<h4>Dignity of all</h4>
<p>This lesson has profound social implications: if every person is the image of God, then abortion, euthanasia, discrimination, extreme poverty, and all forms of violence against human beings are offenses against God. Invite participants to connect faith with social realities.</p>

<h4>Sensitivity with wounds</h4>
<p>Some participants may have a very negative self-image. The phrase &laquo;you are the image of God&raquo; can be revolutionary for them. Be especially kind and patient. Do not force anyone to share if they do not want to.</p>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>&#x1F331; Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use the story of the crumpled coin. Activity: give each child a small mirror and tell them: &laquo;God sees you and loves you.&raquo; Draw what it means to be the image of God. Memorize Gn 1:27 with gestures.</p>

<h4>&#x1F33F; Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connect with their struggle for identity and acceptance. Social media, peer pressure, and beauty standards challenge their dignity. Ask: &laquo;Where do you get your sense of value: from likes or from God?&raquo;</p>

<h4>&#x1FAB4; Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults need to heal their self-image. Help them distinguish between the world's message (&laquo;you are worth what you do&raquo;) and God's message (&laquo;you are worth what you are&raquo;). They can share experiences of how faith has transformed their self-perception.</p>

<h4>&#x1F333; Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the theology of &laquo;divinization&raquo; in the Church Fathers. Discuss the implications of human dignity for Catholic social teaching: the common good, universal destination of goods, preferential option for the poor.</p>
</section>

