<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4>&laquo;Isn't evolution incompatible with man being created in the image of God?&raquo;</h4>
<p>Not necessarily. The Church does not oppose evolution as an explanation for the origin of the human body, but affirms that the spiritual soul is created directly by God (CCC 1705). In any evolutionary process, there is a moment when God infuses a human soul, and that moment marks the creation of a unique person made in his image.</p>

<h4>&laquo;What about people who do not believe in God? Do they still have dignity?&raquo;</h4>
<p>Absolutely. Human dignity does not depend on faith in God. All people, whether believers or not, are created in the image of God and possess the same dignity. The Church defends the human rights of all, regardless of religion. &laquo;Respect for the human person entails respect for the rights that flow from his dignity as a creature&raquo; (CCC 1930).</p>

<h4>&laquo;How can a person who has gravely sinned retain their dignity?&raquo;</h4>
<p>Sin does not destroy the image of God, it darkens it. Even the worst sinner retains their fundamental dignity. That is why the Church calls the sinner to conversion: not to recover their dignity (which they never lost), but to live in accordance with it. God never stops loving anyone, no matter how far they have gone.</p>

<h4>&laquo;What implications does human dignity have for the unborn?&raquo;</h4>
<p>From conception, the human being is a person and possesses all the dignity of a being created in the image of God. The Catechism is clear: &laquo;Human life must be respected and protected absolutely from the moment of conception&raquo; (CCC 2270). This is not only Catholic doctrine but a truth of reason: there is no moment in pregnancy when the human being &laquo;becomes&raquo; a person; they are one from the beginning.</p>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Father, you who created me in your image,<br />
thank you for the gift of life and dignity.<br />
Help me to see your face in every person I meet,<br />
especially those the world considers insignificant.<br />
Give me words to convey the truth of our dignity,<br />
and a heart that knows how to welcome the wounded.<br />
May my life be a reflection of your creative and redeeming love.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>
</section>

